PH('Olbers Paradox: Why Is the Night Sky Dark',
   'Grade 12 Physics strand: Olbers paradox asks why the night sky is dark rather than uniformly bright, given that in an infinite, static universe filled evenly with stars every line of sight should eventually terminate on a stars surface, a puzzle resolved by the universes finite age and the expansion revealed by cosmological redshift.',
   [('What observational fact does Olbers paradox attempt to explain?', ['Why the night sky appears dark rather than uniformly bright', 'Why the daytime sky appears blue rather than another colour', 'Why stars appear to twinkle rather than shine steadily', 'Why the Moon appears to change shape over the course of a month'], 0),
    ('Under the assumption of an infinite, static, evenly star-filled universe, what would every line of sight from Earth eventually be expected to hit?', ['The surface of some star, making the entire sky appear bright', 'Empty space with absolutely no light reaching Earth at all', 'A single, unique star directly overhead only', 'Nothing at all, since light could never reach Earth in such a universe'], 0),
    ('Which two features of the actual universe help resolve Olbers paradox?', ['The universes finite age and its ongoing cosmic expansion', 'The complete absence of any stars in the universe', 'The fact that all stars are located at exactly the same distance from Earth', 'The claim that light from stars never actually reaches Earth'], 0),
    ('How does cosmological redshift, arising from the universes expansion, contribute to explaining the darkness of the night sky?', ['It shifts light from very distant, receding sources toward longer wavelengths, reducing the visible brightness reaching Earth', 'Redshift makes every distant star appear brighter than it otherwise would', 'Redshift has no connection whatsoever to the observed darkness of the sky', 'Redshift only affects sound waves, not light from distant stars'], 0),
    ('Why is Olbers paradox considered an important historical puzzle in cosmology?', ['Resolving it required insights into the finite age and expansion of the universe, ideas central to modern cosmological models', 'It was resolved without any reference to the age or expansion of the universe', 'It has no connection to how modern cosmological models were developed', 'It was resolved by proving that stars do not actually emit any light'], 0)]),
